Kern finds all the organising and naming and workflow confusing so he has turned to his best friend "ChatGPT"-kun and created a 'brief'(subjective) guideline 

1. Branching
  To keep our main codebase stable, never commit directly to the main (or master) branch. 
  Always create a new branch for your work. (code/updates)

  Branch Naming Convention
    Name your branches clearly so everyone knows what you are working on:
      feature/feature-name (e.g., feature/user-login)
      bugfix/issue-name (e.g., bugfix/broken-submit-button)
      docs/topic (e.g., docs/update-readme

2. The Git Workflow
  - Sync your local machine
  - Create Branch
  - Track your changes consistently 
    (Best not to wait until the end of the week to commit. Commit small, logical chunks of work frequently is the best practice.)
  - Push to the repository
  - Open a Pull Request  
    (Once feature is ready, open a PR to merge it into main. Ask at least one teammate for a code review/let team know, if we are in a rush.)

3. Commit Message Guidelines
  Your commit messages should follow this format: type: brief description of change
  Common Types:
    feat: A new feature (e.g., feat: add password reset option)
    fix: A bug fix (e.g., fix: resolve crash on mobile layout)
    docs: Documentation changes only (e.g., docs: update setup instructions)
    style: Changes that do not affect the meaning of the code (white-space, formatting, etc.)
    refactor: A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature (improving code structure)

  Good Example: feat: add email verification to signup page
  Bad Example: fix: fixed some stuff or working code

4. Code Quality Standards
  - Readability
    Intentional Naming: Variable and function names should explain what they do. (Use isUserLoggedIn instead of flag).
    Formatting: Use consistent indentation and spacing.
    Comments: Write comments to explain why something was done a certain way, not what the code does (the code itself should be clear enough to explain the what).

  - Reusability
    Don't Repeat Yourself (DRY): If you find yourself copying and pasting the same code blocks in multiple places, extract that code into a separate, reusable function or component.
    Single Responsibility: A FUNCTION should do one thing and do it well.

  - Configurability
    No Hardcoding: Never hardcode values that might change (like API URLs, secret keys, or specific timeout thresholds).
    Use Configuration Files: Pull these values into environment variables (.env) or a central configuration file. This allows our app to run smoothly across different environments (Development, Testing, Production).


